  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\n19Z water vapor imagery showed a couple disorganized shortwaves \nlifting northeast into the northern and central plains. A longer \nwavelength trough was setup over the Pacific Northwest. At the \nsurface an inverted trough of low pressure extended from a surface \nlow over northeast NM into south central NEB. Southerly flow and good\ninsolation has kept a very warm airmass across much of the forecast \narea. Although moisture return has been limited to a narrow axis \nalong the inverted trough axis. \n\nFor tonight through Tuesday night, the weak upper disturbance with \nthe surface boundary in the area should provide enough vertical \nmotion for off an on shower activity. The showers have been \nweakening this afternoon, but are expected to redevelop this evening \nas the low level jet and warm air advection pattern picks back up. \nOverall instability and mid level lapse rates are pretty marginal so \nstrong thunderstorms are unlikely. Looks like there may be a break \nin the shower activity Tuesday afternoon and evening with the wave \nmoving east of the area. But renewed warm air advection and \nisentropic lift could cause popcorn showers to form Wednesday \nmorning. \n\nBetter chances for showers and storms are forecast for Friday as \nmodels show a better defined shortwave lifting through the central \nplains. The NBM seems to handle this well. By early next week models \ntry to bring another upper trough out of the western U.S. However \nensembles show spread in the potential outcomes increases quite a \nlot by this time. So with lower predictability, have stuck with the \nNBM/WPC forecast. In short the overall pattern keeps the mean \nwesterlies across the northern tier of the country. This favors \nabove normal temps with occasional precip chances to continue \nthrough the end of this week.",
